# Push–pull substituent design of fullerene dimer at the buried interface toward stable and efficient perovskite solar cells

ABSTRACT Defective interface contact is detrimental to the performance of perovskite solar cells (PSCs), by inducing hysteresis, reducing power conversion efficiency (PCE) and deteriorating operation stability. Interlayer materials are commonly utilized to eliminate interfacial defects; however the design of efficient molecules with superior charge transport and defect passivation capabilities remains a big challenge. Herein, four novel fullerene dimers $\mathbf{DC_{60}}\mathbf{-R1}\mathbf{-R}2$ $\mathbf{R1}=$ H or Cl, $\mathbf{R}2=\mathbf{H}$ or MeO) are synthesized as the interlayers between metal oxides and perovskites. The polar substituents of these fullerene dimers are found to determine the intermolecular interaction between these fullerene molecules, with $\mathbf{DC_{60}-C l-M e O}$ showing the greatest intermolecular charge transport and passivation ability due to the strongest push– pull effect of the electron-accepting chloride $(-\mathbf{Cl})$ and electron-donating methoxy (–MeO). The non-encapsulated planar $(\mathbf{FAPbI_{3}})_{x}(\mathbf{MAPbBr}_{3})_{1-x}$ PSC with $\mathbf{DC_{60}-C l-M e O}$ as the interlayer delivers a maximum PCE of $23.3\%$ with no hysteresis, maintaining nearly $100\%$ of the initial efficiency after being stored in a high humidity environment over ${\bf500h}$ . The extrapolated $\ensuremath{T_{\mathrm{{s80}}}}$ lifetime (the time required to reach $80\%$ of initial performance) of the PSC device could be extended to $\mathbf{1110\h}$ at the maximum power point operation condition under one sun illumination without any encapsulation or inert gas protection, superior to the pure $\mathbf{SnO}_{2}$ -based device $(\mathbf{0.5\:h})$ . Fullerene derivatives with push-pull substituents are therefore promising candidates as the interfacial materials for PSCs to receive high performance.
Keywords: perovskite solar cells, contact interface, passivation, push–pull effects
## INTRODUCTION
The past ten years have witnessed a rapid increase of the power conversion efficiency (PCE) of perovskite solar cells (PSCs) from $3.8\%$ to $26.1\%$ [1]. Contemporary PSCs have a sandwich structure, which is composed of a perovskite light absorber between metal oxide-based electron-transporting layer (ETL) and organic semiconductor-based hole-transporting layer (HTL). Within this sandwich structure, the buried interface between ETL/perovskite plays a critical role in determining the efficiency and stability of PSCs [2–4]. This interface is not only responsible for efficient electron extraction from perovskite to ETL [5,6], but also affects the perovskite crystal quality and trap formation $[7-$ 9]. Different from the mesoporous metal oxide ETLs sintered at high temperatures (usually ${>}450^{\circ}\mathrm{C})$ , planar metal oxide ETLs obtained at low temperatures (usually $\angle150^{\circ}\mathrm{C})$ are promising ETLs to reduce energy cost and are also compatible with the fabrication of flexible devices [8]. However, interfacial modification [10,11] is usually required for planar ETL to obtain sufficient electron extraction capability and minimize carrier accumulation and recombination, thus eliminating hysteresis and long-term degradation [12–16].
Passivating deleterious defects in the bulk and at the interfaces of PSCs are therefore essential in the PSC community [17–21]. $\mathrm{C}_{60}$ and [6,6]-phenyl- $C_{61}$ -butyric acid methyl ester (PCBM) derivatives are typical ETLs or interface layers in p-i-n- and n-i$\boldsymbol{\mathrm{\tt~p~}}$ -structured PSCs, providing excellent conductivity and energy level alignment in PSCs. However, the defect passivation abilities of $\mathrm{C}_{60}$ and PCBM derivatives are unsatisfying due to the limited moieties that can be attached outside the cage of $\mathrm{C}_{60}$ [22–26]. Achieving multifunctionality in fullerene derivatives is a promising way to develop superior ETLs and interface materials for PSCs, but it still lacks efficient core structures to grow various substituents from the $C_{60}$ cage, and a deep understanding of intramolecular and intermolecular interactions among those substituents.
Organic structures with strong electron donating (push) and accepting (pull) properties have been employed to design various organic semiconductors with the push–pull effect, which can tune the energy levels, band gaps and intermolecular charge transfer (CT) [27–31]. We recently have extended the chemical groups outside $C_{60}$ based on fulleropyrrolidine molecules, and a range of fullerene monomers and dimers have been reported [32,33]. However, push–pull moieties have barely been introduced outside the cage of $\mathrm{C}_{60}$ and further employed as the interfacial materials in PSCs. The push–pull moieties can fine tune the energy levels at the ETL/perovskite interface, modulate intermolecular dipole-dipole interactions to adjust charge mobility [31], and ensure multiple passivation of the undercoordinated defects [28], and thus are very promising for enhancing the performance of PSCs.
Here, we designed and synthesized a series of new fulleropyrrolidine dimers from $\mathrm{C}_{60}$ with different functional moieties varying in electron pushing and pulling capability, and employed them as interfacial materials to fabricate planar heterojunction PSCs with superior performance. The intensive interactions of push–pull substituents ( $_{-\mathrm{{MeO}}}$ and $-\mathrm{Cl},$ having opposite polarity) contribute to favorable molecular $\pi$ -stacking, leading to significantly enhanced charge mobility and multiple passivation effect toward perovskite, which can be well verified by density functional theory (DFT) simulation. As a result, our glass/indium tin oxide $\mathrm{(ITO)/SnO_{2}/D C_{60}\mathrm{-Cl\mathrm{-}M e O/}}$ perovskite/ Spiro-OMeTAD/Ag $(2,2^{\prime},7,7^{\prime}$ -tetrakis[N,N-di(4-methoxyphenyl) amino]-9-9'-spirobifluorene (spiro-OMeTAD)) planar PSC delivers a maximum PCE of $23.3\%$ without hysteresis. It also maintains nearly $100\%$ of the initial efficiency undergoing a $500\mathrm{-h}$ storage in high humidity and exhibits an extrapolated $T_{\mathrm{S80}}$ lifetime (the time required to reach $80\%$ of initial performance) of over $1000\mathrm{h}$ under maximum power point (MPP) tracking, one sun illumination without any encapsulation or inert gas protection.
## EXPERIMENTAL SECTION
## Theoretical simulation
The DFT method and the generalized gradient approximation (GGA) with the Perdew–Burke–Ernzerhof (PBE) exchangecorrelation functional were used to compute the molecular orbitals, electrostatic potential (ESP) distribution and dipole moments based on the optimized geometries of the fullerene dimers simulated by Forcite and DMol3 modules in Materials Studio software. The atomistic molecular dynamics (MD) simulations were performed using the amorphous cell module. The atom types and intra- and inter-molecular interaction parameters of fullerene dimers were built based on the ESP population assignment after molecular optimization. The thinfilm molecular packing structures of fullerenes were obtained by constructing amorphous cells with $0.5\mathrm{g}\mathrm{cm}^{-3}$ density containing 20 molecules with different conformations and then optimized. The timescale of the MD simulations was 100 ps and the force field was compass II. The $\pi{-}\pi$ stacking frequency was calculated from the optimized configuration of fullerene molecules at the final stage in MD simulations. Adsorption configurations were simulated by adsorption locator modules for interactions between fullerene dimers and $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ as well as perovskites.
## Device fabrication
## RESULTS AND DISCUSSION
DFT simulations were performed to characterize the electron structure and surface EPS distribution of these four fullerene dimers to demonstrate the push–pull effect of different substituents. All fullerene dimers exhibit excellent electron delocalization (Fig. S1) and suitable band structure (Table 1), verifying that they are excellent candidates as the interlayer materials in PSCs [33,34]. The MeO moiety exhibits a positive EPS owing to its electron donating property (Fig. 1b), leading to a dipole rise from 0.4 Debye of the benzene unit (BZ) to 1.2 Debye of BZ– MeO. Meanwhile, $-\mathrm{Cl}$ exhibits strong electron accepting ability, increasing the dipole of BZ–Cl to $-2.1$ Debye (minus dipoles represent the opposite direction to positive dipoles) [35,36]. All three substituents introduce enrichments of positive and negative charges, while larger dipoles contribute more to charge delocalization, enhancing the intermolecular interaction between fullerene dimers. Moreover, $\mathrm{DC}_{60}{-}\mathrm{H-MeO}$ exhibits a slightly increased highest occupied molecular orbital (HOMO) level and decreased band gap compared with $\mathrm{DC}_{60}{-}\mathrm{H-H}$ , which is caused by the weak electron-donating nature of –MeO (Fig. 1d and Table 1) [37]. The Cl substituent on R1 leads to an obvious drop of the lowest unoccupied molecular orbital (LUMO) energy level in both $\mathrm{DC}_{60}\mathrm{-Cl\mathrm{-}H}$ and $\mathrm{DC}_{60}{-}\mathrm{Cl-MeO}$ , which illustrates the strong electron accepting ability of Cl unit and might facilitate the charge extraction from perovskite to ETL due to the reduced energy barrier.
The UV-visible (vis) absorption spectra of four fullerene dimers are shown in Fig. 1c, where the main excitation of $\mathrm{DC}_{60}-$ $_\mathrm{H-H}$ at around $340~\mathrm{nm}$ is similar to PCBM and other fullerene derivatives. The electron-donating unit in $\mathrm{DC}_{60}{-}\mathrm{H-MeO}$ causes a red shift of the main excitation to ca. $380\mathrm{nm}$ and significantly enhanced CT bands in the visible light region, which is consistent with the decreased band gap compared with $\mathrm{DC}_{60}\mathrm{-H-H}$ . For the –Cl substituted fullerene dimers, their high energy excitations at ca. $310\mathrm{nm}$ and broad low energy excitations illustrate strong CT from other conjugated moieties to $\mathrm{BZ\mathrm{-}C l}$ [38]. Besides, $\mathrm{DC_{60}-C l-M e O}$ shows excellent solvent resistance to dimethyl formamide (DMF), helping to maintain the interlayer during the sequential solution-casting of the perovskite layer for effective interfacial modification (Fig. S2).
We also calculated the frequency of $\pi{-}\pi$ stacking and average stacking distance from twenty fullerene dimers to evaluate the electron hopping probability (Fig. 2f and Fig. S3). The $\pi-\pi$ stacking frequency follows the order of $\mathrm{DC_{60}-C l-M e O}$ $(85\%)>$ $\mathrm{DC}_{60}\mathrm{-Cl\mathrm{-}H}$ $(60\%)>\mathrm{DC}_{60}–\mathrm{H-MeO}$ $(50\%)>\mathrm{DC}_{60}–H–H\ (0\%),$ which is consistent with the trend of dipole moments of substituents and the experimental results of conductivity and mobility. The average stacking distances among fullerene molecules are as follows: PCBM $\breve{(3.0\mathrm{\AA})}$ , $\mathrm{C}_{60}$ $(2.8\mathring\mathrm{\textrm{A}})$ , $\mathrm{\bar{D}C_{60}-H-H}$ $(3.1\mathring\mathrm{A})$ , $\mathrm{DC}_{60}{-}\mathrm{H-MeO}$ $(3.0\mathring\mathrm{A})$ , $\mathrm{DC}_{60}\mathrm{-Cl-H}$ $(2.9\mathring\mathrm{A})$ , $\mathrm{DC}_{60}$ –Cl– MeO $(2.7\mathrm{\AA})$ . $\mathrm{DC}_{60}$ –Cl–MeO shows the lowest average distance between molecules, contributing to its superior mobility. Specifically, $\mathrm{DC_{60}-C l-M e O}$ shows the highest mobility $(6.8~\times}$ $10^{-3}\thinspace\mathrm{cm}^{2}\thinspace\mathrm{V}^{-1}\thinspace\mathrm{s}^{-1})$ and conductivity $(6.1\ \mathrm{\stackrel{\cdot}{\times}~}10^{-4}\mathrm{~S~m^{-1}})$ , which is about ten and two times higher than the mobility $(0.7~\times$ $10^{-3}\thinspace\mathrm{cm}^{2}\mathrm{V}^{-1}\thinspace s^{-1})$ and conductivity $(2.6\times10^{-4}\mathrm{S}\mathrm{m}^{-1})$ of $\mathrm{DC}_{60}-$
H–H. These values are also higher than the mobility of $4.3\ \times$ $10^{-3}\thinspace\mathrm{cm}^{2}\thinspace\mathrm{V}^{-1}\thinspace s^{-1}$ and conductivity of $1.7\times10^{-4}\mathrm{S}\ \mathrm{m}^{-1}$ for PCBM [33]. This illustrates that the interaction between positive and negative charge enrichment regions caused by the push–pull substituents in the fullerene dimers is key to their charge transport properties [42,43]. Constructing all-in-one fullerene derivatives with the co-existence of push and pull substituents can be an efficient molecular design protocol to modulate their $\pi{-}\pi$ stacking and charge transport abilities.
PSCs with a structure of $\mathrm{ITO}/\mathrm{SnO}_{2}/$ fullerene dimers/ $\scriptstyle({\mathrm{FAPbI}}_{3})_{x}({\mathrm{MAPbBr}}_{3})_{1-x}/,$ Spiro-OMeTAD/Ag were fabricated to demonstrate the influence of fullerene dimers on device performance. Our control device without any interlayer exhibits a maximum PCE of $17.7\%$ in forward scan, far lower than that in reverse scan $(20.4\%)$ (Fig. 3a). This severe hysteresis ( $\Delta{\mathrm{PCE}}=$ $2.7\%$ can be reduced to different level with four different fullerene interlayers, specifically $\mathrm{DC}_{60}\mathrm{-H-H}$ $\langle\Delta\mathrm{PCE}=0.9\%$ ), $\mathrm{DC}_{60}-$ H–MeO $\langle\Delta\mathrm{PCE}=1.0\%$ ), $\mathrm{DC}_{60}\mathrm{-Cl-H}$ ( $\langle\Delta\mathrm{PCE}=0.9\%\rangle$ and $\mathrm{DC}_{60}-$ Cl–MeO $(\Delta\mathrm{PCE}=0\%$ ), as shown in Fig. 3a, b and Table 2, whose trend completely coincides with the $\pi{-}\pi$ stacking and charge transport abilities. $\mathrm{DC}_{60}\mathrm{-Cl\mathrm{-}H}$ increases the efficiency from $21.1\%$ to $22.8\%$ in reverse scan and from $20.2\%$ to $21.9\%$ in forward scan (Fig. 3b) in contrast to $\mathrm{DC}_{60}\mathrm{-H-H}$ (Fig. 3a), owing to the highly improved fill factor (FF) and open-circuit voltage $(V_{\mathrm{oc}})$ . This can be well explained by the superior charge transport and passivating function from electron-accepting Cl substituents [44]. $\mathrm{DC}_{60}$ –H–MeO (Fig. 3b) exhibits less promotion by increasing the maximum PCE to $21.6\%$ (reverse scan) and
$20.6\%$ (forward scan) with enhanced FF and similar $V_{\mathrm{oc}},$ emphasizing the moderate passivation and charge transport capability of the MeO group. $\mathrm{DC}_{60}{-}\mathrm{Cl-MeO}$ not only eliminates the hysteresis of PSC completely, but also promotes the PCE to the highest level $(\mathrm{PCE}=23.3\%$ , $\mathrm{FF}=82.0\%$ , short-circuit current $J_{\mathrm{sc}}=25.6~\mathrm{mA}~\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ and $V_{\mathrm{oc}}=1.11\:\mathrm{V};$ (Fig. 3b). As a comparison, device performance improvement employing the PCBM interlayer is much less than those raised by the fullerene dimers explored in this work (Fig. S4).
Average device parameters (PCE, $J_{\mathrm{sc}}$ , $V_{\mathrm{{oc}}}$ and FF) obtained from 15 individual devices are plotted in Fig. 3c–f and summarized in Table 2. The average $J_{\mathrm{sc}}$ of PSCs containing the fullerene dimer interlayer increases compared with the $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ - based device without any interlayer (Fig. 3d). For the best-performing devices, the deviation between $J_{\mathrm{sc}}$ and integrated current density calculated from external quantum efficiency (EQE) results (Fig. S5) is less than $5\%$ , proving the reliability of our current density-voltage $\left(J\cdot W\right)$ tests [45]. In terms of the difference between different fullerene interlayer-based devices, the $J_{\mathrm{sc}}$ variation is much less compared with those of FF and $V_{\mathrm{oc}},$ suggesting that any changes toward the bulk perovskite layer, i.e. the improvements of grain size (Fig. S6), crystallization (Fig. S7) and light absorption (Fig. S8) of perovskite films grown on hydrophobic fullerene interlayer, play the secondary role whilst the interfacial property plays the primary role. To elucidate the effect of fullerene dimers on the charge extraction from perovskite to $\mathrm{SnO}_{2};$ we investigated the steady-state photoluminescence (PL) of $\left(\mathrm{FAPbI_{3}}\right)_{x}(\mathrm{MAPbBr}_{3})_{1-x}$ perovskite and found that the PL quenching effects (Fig. S9) followed $\mathrm{DC}_{60}$ –Cl– MeO > $\mathrm{DC_{60}\mathrm{-Cl\mathrm{-}H}\mathrm{~>~\mathrm{DC_{60}\mathrm{-}H\mathrm{-}M e O}~>~\mathrm{DC_{60}\mathrm{-}H\mathrm{-}H}~>~\mathrm{SnO_{2},}}}$ explaining the FF enhancement in $J{-}V$ tests [46]. Therefore, it is the charge transport and recombination process rather than the enhancement of perovskite qualities that is critical for device performance, consistent with our device results.
We further characterized the operational stability of our PSCs in continuous illumination of xenon lamp (containing UV light) at MPP under $100\mathrm{mW}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ (Fig. 3g, Figs S10 and S11). The $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ -based device exhibits obvious burn-in phenomenon during the initial $100\ s$ and then stabilizes at $19.5\%$ (Fig. S10), with an abrupt failure in a longer time scale (in Fig. 3g). This abrupt failure might be triggered by the photocatalytic property of metal oxide under UV light that accelerates the decomposition of perovskite. In contrast, the $\mathrm{DC}_{60}\mathrm{-Cl-H-}$ and $\mathrm{DC}_{60}{\mathrm{-Cl-MeO-}}$ 一 modified PSCs illustrate similar PCEs in both forward and reverse scans (FS and RS) and good operational stability with no obvious PCE decreasing in $600\ s$ (Fig. S10). $\mathrm{DC}_{60}\mathrm{-H-H-}$ and $\mathrm{DC}_{60}$ –H–MeO-based PSCs decay slightly during this period, which is consistent with the trend of hysteresis. The extra UVlight absorption and passivation capability of fullerene dimer interlayers protect perovskite from UV-light induced degradation during operation. We have also compared the UV absorbance of PCBM and $\mathrm{DC}_{60}\mathrm{-H-H}$ after UV light illumination for $15\mathrm{h}$ in Fig. S12. It turned out that PCBM showed significantly decreased absorbance in $15\mathrm{h}$ while the absorbance of ${\mathrm{DC}}_{60}{-}{\mathrm{H}}{-}$ H maintained in the same condition, which reveals the superior stability of our fullerene dimers under UV light. Our champion device (without any encapsulation and inert gas protection) based on $\mathrm{DC}_{60}{-}\mathrm{Cl-MeO}$ shows no attenuation of the initial PCE in the first $4000\mathrm{~s~}$ and exhibits an extrapolated $T_{\mathrm{S80}}$ lifetime of $1110\pm50\mathrm{h}$ , exceeding that of pure $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ based device $(0.5\mathrm{h})$ by three orders of magnitude (Fig. $3\mathrm{g}$ and Fig. S11). Non-encapsulated device based on $\mathrm{DC}_{60}{-}\mathrm{Cl-MeO}$ also undergoes a longterm stability test in a humidity environment (from $50\%$ to $70\%$ ) for 22 days and no decline of PCE appears (Fig. 3h).
To elucidate the difference of passivation effect caused by the substituents on fullerene dimers, adsorption configuration and adsorption energy $(E_{\mathrm{ads}})$ of fullerene dimers on $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ and perovskite surfaces were simulated by adsorption locator module of Materials Studio and shown in Fig. 4a–h. A closer adsorption configuration and more negative $E_{\mathrm{ads}}$ reflect stronger interaction [33]. Relatively weak adsorption dominates the interaction between the fullerene dimers and $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ , producing similar $E_{\mathrm{ads}}$ at around $-1.4\times10^{3}\mathrm{kcalmol^{-1}}$ (Fig. 4a–c) which is much smaller than those for fullerene dimer/perovskite (Fig. 4e–h), indicating the primary passivation of fullerene dimers toward perovskite at the interface. Interestingly, $\mathrm{DC}_{60}{-}\mathrm{Cl-MeO}$ shows the largest $E_{\mathrm{ads}}$ between metal oxide $(-1.6~\times~10^{3}~\mathrm{kcal~mol^{-1}})$ and perovskite $(-3.8\times10^{3}\mathrm{kcalmol^{-1}},$ , due to more passivation sites of the push–pull substituents on $\mathrm{DC}_{60}\mathrm{-Cl-MeO}$ (Fig. 4d, h).
The defects of the perovskite film include electron-deficient defects (e.g., undercoordinated $\mathrm{Pb}^{2+}$ ) or electron-rich defects (e.g., undercoordinated $\mathrm{I}^{-}$ or $\mathrm{\mathbf{P}b-I}$ anti-site defects), which can be passivated by coordination with Lewis bases and acids [47]. Among all substitutions of these fullerene dimers, –MeO can serve as electron donor while –Cl exhibits electron accepting ability for perovskite defects, which has been well displayed in the adsorption configurations where –MeO (Fig. 4f) and $-\mathrm{Cl}$ (Fig. 4g, h) moieties have mostly inserted into the perovskite lattice [48]. The $E_{\mathrm{ads}}$ of $\mathrm{DC}_{60}{-}\mathrm{H-H}$ on perovskite decreases from $-2.7\times10^{3}$ to $-3.4\times10^{3}\mathrm{kcalmol^{-1}}$ of $\mathrm{DC}_{60}\mathrm{-H-MeO}$ , owing to the –MeO substituent. With the –Cl substituent, the $E_{\mathrm{ads}}$ of $\mathrm{DC}_{60}\mathrm{-Cl-H}$ and $\mathrm{DC}_{60}{-}\mathrm{Cl-MeO}$ can be further decreased to $-3.8$ $\times~10^{3}~\mathrm{kcal~mol^{-1}}$ (Fig. 4g, h) [49]. Therefore, $\mathrm{DC}_{60}{-}\mathrm{Cl-MeO}$ is the most promising interlayer whose interface configuration is the best for passivating both perovskite and metal oxide.
A schematic visualizing the push–pull moieties of fullerene dimers and their corresponding $\pi{-}\pi$ stacking and passivation capability is shown in Fig. 4i. Push or pull substituents with large dipoles can induce strong positive (blue) or negative (red) charge enrichment regions, raising passivation capability to the Lewis acid or base defects of perovskite and metal oxide. The coexistence of push–pull substituents can form polydentate passivation configuration with both perovskite and metal oxide, which is more favorable than monodentate passivation configuration. Moreover, the enrichment regions of positive and negative charges induced by push–pull effects bring the strongest dipole–dipole interactions, facilitating parallel $\pi{-}\pi$ stacking and intermolecular charge transport. Fullerene derivatives with the existence of push–pull substituents are therefore a promising candidate as interfacial materials for PSCs to receive high performance.
## CONCLUSION
To sum up, we claimed a series of superior interlayer materials for PSCs by introducing push–pull substituents into fullerene dimers. Dipole–dipole enhanced intermolecular interaction from these push–pull substituents not only regulates the $\pi$ - stacking configuration but also influences the passivation capability of the fullerene dimers, which promotes electron extraction and reduces interface losses for PSCs. The co-existence of –Cl and –MeO in fullerene dimer $\mathrm{DC}_{60}{-}\mathrm{Cl-MeO}$ leads to the highest photovoltaic properties of our non-encapsulated glass/ $\mathrm{ITO/SnO_{2}/D C_{60}-C l-M e O/}$ perovskite/Spiro-OMeTAD/Ag planar PSC, boosting the PCE to $23.3\%$ without hysteresis. The champion device can maintain nearly $100\%$ of the initial efficiency after a relatively high humidity storage of over $500\mathrm{{h}}$ , and its extrapolated $T_{\mathrm{S80}}$ lifetime is extended to $1110\mathrm{h}$ when operating under continuous illumination of xenon lamp $(100\mathrm{mW}\mathrm{cm}^{-2},$ ) at MPP without encapsulation or inert gas protection.
